  "Question": "Let \\(a,\\) \\(b,\\) \\(c,\\) \\(d,\\) and \\(e\\) be five consecutive terms in an arithmetic sequence, and suppose that \\(a+b+c+d+e=30.\\) Which of the following can be found?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(a\\)",
    "B": "\\(b\\)",
    "C": "\\(c\\)",
    "D": "\\(d\\)",
    "E": "\\(e\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Equal spacing makes the terms on opposite sides of \\(c\\) balance: \\(a\\) and \\(e\\) are equally far from \\(c\\), as are \\(b\\) and \\(d\\). Consequently,\n\\[\na+e=2c,\\qquad b+d=2c.\n\\]\nThe given sum becomes\n\\[\n30=(a+e)+(b+d)+c=5c,\n\\]\nso \\(c=6\\), regardless of the common difference.\n\nThe other terms are not fixed. For example, \\(4,5,6,7,8\\) and \\(2,4,6,8,10\\) are both arithmetic sequences with sum \\(30\\), but they differ at every position except the middle. Thus only \\(c\\) can be found, choice C.",
